## The Catch-All: `_`

The underscore `_` in a `match` expression is the **wildcard pattern**. It matches ANYTHING.

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let number = 42;

  let result = match number {
    0 => "zero",
    1 => "one",
    _ => "something else",  // Catches everything except 0 and 1
  };

  io.println(result);  // prints: something else
  return 0;
}
```

### Why _ is Essential

`match` must be **exhaustive** — every possible value must be handled. Without `_`, you'd need to list every possible Int value (all ~18 quintillion of them!).

```xiom
// This WON'T compile — not all Int values are covered!
// match x {
//   1 => "one",
//   2 => "two",
// }  // ERROR: non-exhaustive match!

// This WILL compile — _ handles everything else
match x {
  1 => "one",
  2 => "two",
  _ => "other",  // All good!
}
```

### _ Means "I Don't Care"

Use `_` when you genuinely don't care about the value:

```xiom
match status_code {
  200 => "OK",
  404 => "Not Found",
  _ => "Other status",  // I don't care about the specific code
}
```

### _ Must Come Last

The wildcard catches everything, so it must be the LAST arm:

```xiom
// WRONG: _ first catches everything
match x {
  _ => "other",  // This matches EVERYTHING
  1 => "one",    // DEAD CODE — never reached!
}

// CORRECT: _ is the fallback
match x {
  1 => "one",
  2 => "two",
  _ => "other",  // Only runs if x is not 1 or 2
}
```

### Exhaustiveness for Specific Types

Some types have a small number of values — the compiler knows this:

```xiom
// Bool has only two values — both must be covered
match flag {
  true => "yes",
  false => "no",
}
// No _ needed — all possible Bool values are covered!
```

But for `Int`, `Str`, `Float64` — types with infinite values — you almost always need `_`.

